Lib/rt -- the Kairo runtime
===========================

The C the builtin library calls. Installed beside builtin as
<resource-dir>/rt (the runtime root is the builtin root's parent), and
compiled into every link that emitted Kairo objects, on the same cc1 line
as Kairo's own TUs (CompilerInstance::_compile_runtime).

Layout
------

Flat, one file per function family, the way compiler-rt's lib/builtins is.
The driver compiles EVERY .c/.cc/.cpp directly in this directory
(CompilerInstance::_runtime_sources enumerates it, sorted), so a new
source file is picked up with no driver change; a header or this README
is never compiled. Each object is <cache>/kairo/<stem>.o, so stems must be
unique, and a subdirectory is NOT searched.

    crash.h            public: kairo_rt_crash (+ _exit, signal), the crash path
    intrinsics.h       header-only kairo_rt_* helpers behind base/intrinsics.k
    crash.c              the one entry; async-signal-safe (POSIX, Windows,
                         wasm)
    global_alloc.h     public: kairo_global_*, the global allocator's ABI
    global_alloc.c       their weak System definitions; the file that sets
                         @set_global_allocator defines them strongly
    fmt.h              public: the heap and float text FmtBuf calls
    fmt_impl.h           internal to fmt_*.c: shared types (u128, db_dec,
                         fval, fclass, bn, exact_ws), the Dragonbox
                         helpers, and the cross-file declarations
    fmt_alloc.c          kairo_rt_fmt_alloc / realloc / free
    fmt_dragonbox32.c    shortest digits, binary32 (with its cache table)
    fmt_dragonbox64.c    shortest digits, binary64 (with its cache table)
    fmt_exact.c          the bignum; exact shortest and fixed digits
    fmt_decode.c         a bit pattern -> fclass (IEEE shapes, x87)
    fmt_layout.c         digits -> text, and format_float over all of it
    fmt_float.c          the kairo_rt_fmt_<format> entry points

Headers
-------

One public header per area, because the builtin imports a header, not a
function: `ffi "c" import "rt/crash.h"` (errors/crash.k) and
`ffi "c++" import "rt/fmt.h"` (fmt/fmt_buf.k). Splitting the .c files does
not change what the builtin imports. Everything a family shares with its
siblings goes in an internal <area>_impl.h that only Lib/rt includes; the
builtin never imports one. Includes are spelled from Lib/ ("rt/fmt.h"):
the driver puts the runtime root on the include path of both the runtime
and the builtin's ffi header sets.

Names
-----

Everything with external linkage is kairo_rt_*: these objects link into
user programs, so an unprefixed name would collide with the user's. The
one exception is kairo_global_* (global_alloc.h): those names are an ABI
the compiler writes strong definitions of, not the runtime's own. A
helper one file calls in another is kairo_rt_<area>_<name>_ (the trailing
underscore marks it internal: declared in <area>_impl.h, never in a public
header); a helper used in one file stays `static`.

ABI
---

Two layouts here are fixed across compiler versions, because objects built
by different Kairo releases (and hand-written C) meet at them. The user-
facing statement is allocators.mdx, "The global allocator ABI".

  - The kairo_global_* symbols (global_alloc.h): five C-linkage functions,
    their names, argument order and types. global_alloc.c defines them
    weakly as System; the file that writes @set_global_allocator(A)
    defines them strongly as forwarders to A (the compiler writes those).
  - ScopeFrame (Lib/builtin/base/alloc.k): one open scoped allocator,
    seven pointer-sized fields in this order, no padding:
        alloc    unsigned char *(*)(size_t size, size_t align)
        free     void (*)(unsigned char *ptr, size_t size, size_t align)
        resize   bool (*)(unsigned char *ptr, size_t old, size_t new, size_t align)
        realloc  unsigned char *(*)(unsigned char *ptr, size_t old, size_t new, size_t align)
        lo, hi   unsigned char *: the allocator's range() when the scope opened
        prev     ScopeFrame *: the frame below, or null
    linked from the per-thread scope_top (a plain global where there are
    no threads). Reordering, removing or retyping a field breaks every
    object built against the old layout.

Portability
-----------

Must compile under clang for Linux (glibc and musl), Darwin, Windows
(MSVC ABI), wasm/WASI and freestanding targets. So: C17, no compiler
builtin without a fallback (`__int128` and `__builtin_clzll` are both
guarded), no libc formatting (no locale), only <stddef.h>, <stdint.h>,
<float.h>, <stdlib.h> and <string.h> outside crash.c, and per-target code
behind the target's own macro (crash.c's _WIN32 / __wasm__ split,
fmt_float.c's LDBL_MANT_DIG). Tests/Codegen/builtin_fmt_arm64_msvc.k builds
the builtin for aarch64-pc-windows-msvc.

Where a new function goes
-------------------------

  - Part of an existing family: its file.
  - A new family of an existing area: a new <area>_<family>.c, its shared
    declarations in <area>_impl.h, its public entry in <area>.h.
  - A new area: <area>.h plus its .c files, and the builtin file that
    `ffi`-imports <area>.h.
